y markedly decreased endothelin levels. These findings underscore the importance of oxidative and endothelial pathways in the therapeutic action of these agents [Data: Relationships (381, 3062, 310); Entities (13274, 13358)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Adverse effects are documented primarily in preclinical models\",\n            \"explanation\": \"An adverse reaction associated with DanShen Acid B is reduced body weight in rats, particularly when co-administered with 5-fluorouracil. This finding originates from animal studies and highlights potential metabolic or synergistic toxicities in combination therapies. While human adverse event data are not provided in the source material, this preclinical observation warrants caution in polypharmacy contexts, especially in oncology-cardiology intersections. No adverse reactions are reported for TMZ in the provided data, though its long-term safety profile would require further clinical validation [Data: Relationships (8289); Entities (15543, 3378)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"High-density lipoprotein (HDL) is a shared positive biomarker affected by both drug classes\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Both TMZ and DanShen Acid B (along with Paeonol) are associated with increased levels of high-density lipoprotein (HDL), a cardioprotective lipid fraction. Elevated HDL improves reverse cholesterol transport and has anti-inflammatory and antioxidant properties. In the context of heart failure and ischemic conditions, boosting HDL may contribute to plaque stabilization and endothelial protection. This convergence on HDL modulation suggests a common beneficial pathway despite differing primary mechanisms—TMZ acting via metabolic optimization and DanShen compounds via antioxidant and rheological effects [Data: Relationships (123, 386); Entities (14250, 3075, 3378, 6797)].\"\n        }\n    ],\n    \"rating\": 6.5,\n    \"rating_explanation\": \"The community represents clinical